<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=pencil">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pencil</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1560s</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A pencil is a writing or drawing implement with a solid core of pigment, usually graphite, in a protective casing. Around 1560 the Italian couple Simonio and Lyndiana Bernacotti made what are likely the first plans for a wood-encased pencil, from a hollowed stick of juniper. In 1565 a large deposit of pure, solid graphite, then thought to be a form of lead and called plumbago, was found at Seathwaite in Borrowdale, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and its sticks were at first wrapped in string or sheepskin. The first attempt to make graphite sticks from powder was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1662, and in 1795 Nicolas-Jacques Conté fired powdered graphite with clay, the method still in use.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pencil.jpg" alt="Pencil" width="330" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ole Palnatoke Andersen · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014-01-16-Hittegods_(12009261426).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1560s</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Borrowdale, Cumbria, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Simonio Bernacotti · Lyndiana Bernacotti</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some sources date the discovery of the Borrowdale graphite as early as 1500.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=pencil">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
